  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe makes 2 portions and takes about 30 minutes."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Omurice"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "is a"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "wafu"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "classic, a Western omelette idea reimagined in a Japanese kitchen. Inside sits"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "ketchup-rice"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", fried with chicken and vegetables until glossy and rich. The whole thing comes together in one frying pan and needs almost no kit."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Use cold cooked rice from the day before. Freshly cooked rice is too wet and will steam rather than fry, giving a claggy result. Cook the egg on a gentle heat and pull the pan off the moment the omelette is just set but still glossy. Carry-over heat will finish it as you wrap."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "ingredientsList",
        "attrs": {
          "defaultServings": 2,
          "items": [
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "japanese-short-grain-rice",
              "amount": 300,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "cooked and cooled, ideally day-old",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "chicken-thigh",
              "amount": 150,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "skin off, cut into 1cm cubes",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "eggs",
              "amount": 4,
              "unit": "whole",
              "prepNote": "2 per portion",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "vegetable-oil",
              "amount": 30,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "spring-onion",
              "amount": 3,
              "unit": "whole",
              "prepNote": "finely sliced",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "soy-sauce-light",
              "amount": 15,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "sesame-oil",
              "amount": 10,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat 15ml of vegetable oil in a wide frying pan over a high heat. Add the chicken and cook for 3 to 4 minutes, turning, until golden and cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the spring onions to the pan. Stir for 30 seconds. Add the cold rice and break up any clumps with a spatula. Fry for 2 minutes, pressing the rice against the hot pan."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the soy sauce and sesame oil. Toss everything together. The rice should look glossy and slightly coloured. Taste and adjust the seasoning. Divide the rice into 2 portions and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Wipe the pan clean. Heat another 7ml of vegetable oil over a medium-low heat. Beat 2 eggs lightly and pour into the pan. Swirl to coat the base. Cook for about 90 seconds until just set but still slightly runny on top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place one portion of rice in the centre of the omelette. Fold both sides of the omelette over the rice. Slide onto a warmed plate, seam-side down. Repeat for the second portion."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Squeeze a line of ketchup along the top of each omurice. Serve straight away."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The omelette tears when folding",
              "cause": "Egg was overcooked or the pan was too hot",
              "fix": "Use a medium-low heat and pull the pan off the hob the moment the egg is set but still glossy. A non-stick pan helps a great deal here."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The rice is mushy and sticky",
              "cause": "Freshly cooked rice was used or the pan was not hot enough",
              "fix": "Always use cold, day-old rice. Make sure the oil is properly hot before the rice goes in. Spread the rice in a single layer and press it to the pan rather than stirring constantly."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The filling tastes bland",
              "cause": "Not enough seasoning in the rice",
              "fix": "Add an extra splash of soy sauce and a little more sesame oil. Taste the rice before wrapping."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Omurice appeared in Japanese Western-style restaurants called yoshoku around the early 1900s. It is a prime example of wafu cooking, taking a foreign idea and making it distinctly Japanese. Today it is a staple of home cooking and appears on the menu of kissaten coffee shops across the country. Children love it for the bright ketchup topping. Adults love it because it is fast, filling, and uses up leftover rice."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}